  - title: "Legal Recourse and Contractual Armor: Defending Against SCM Vaporware"
    permalink: "https://runink.org/blog/vaporware-part-5-legal/"
    date: "2026-06-20T08:28:51Z"
    category: "blog"
    description: "Part of the 6-part series on the dangers of SCM Vaporware."
    content: " This is Part 5 of our 6-part series on the dangers of SCM Vaporware. Read Part 4: Operational Paralysis: When Phantom SCM Software Breaks the Supply Chain for the operational impact, and conclude with Part 6: Building Genuine Resilience.\nBuying supply chain software is a legal act as much as a technical one. The contract is what you are left holding if the software never works.\nPart 2 covered how to spot vaporware before you sign. This part is about the terms that protect you if you sign anyway — because sometimes the decision is made above you, and the contract is the only place left to put a guard.\nThis article is not legal advice. Use it to brief your own counsel.\nThe Danger of Standard Vendor Contracts The first mistake is signing the vendor\u0026rsquo;s own paperwork as written.\nTheir agreements are drafted to protect them. The language around dates is usually soft. The definition of \u0026ldquo;working\u0026rdquo; is usually vague. The limit on what they owe you if it fails is usually low. Many include a line saying you should not buy on the strength of features that are not released yet — which is exactly how vaporware is sold.\nSign that as it stands and you have agreed, in advance, that the promises made in the room do not count.\nForging Your Contractual Armor: Essential Clauses Four terms do most of the work. Your counsel and your buying team need to put them in before signature, not after the first missed date.\n1. A price for missing the date Vaporware lives on dates that move. So name them, and attach a cost to missing them.\nWrite down each feature, what it must do, and the date it must do it by. Then name the consequence:\nmoney back from fees already paid; a charge for each day late, taken off what you owe next; the right to walk away at no cost. The point is not to collect the penalty. It is that a vendor who cannot build the feature will say so before signing rather than after.\n2. Pay for what passes a test, not for what is promised Never pay the whole licence up front. A vendor who already has the money has no reason to finish.\nTie each payment to a test. Your team runs it, on your data, against the specification in the statement of work. It passes or it does not. If it does not, the payment waits.\n3. Say what the software must actually do Most contracts say the software will perform \u0026ldquo;substantially in accordance with its documentation\u0026rdquo;. That is too loose, because the documentation may be marketing.\nReplace it with the specifics your operation needs. Name the records it must read, the system they come from, how many there are, how long it may take, and what the output has to be. \u0026ldquo;The software will optimise inventory\u0026rdquo; is not testable. \u0026ldquo;The software must read this file from this system and return this, within this time\u0026rdquo; is. Use your own numbers, taken from your own volumes — not the vendor\u0026rsquo;s.\n4. Put the source code in escrow If you are buying a new product from a small vendor, there is a real chance the vendor does not survive the project.\nAsk for a software escrow agreement. The vendor lodges the source code with a neutral third party, and updates it as the software changes. If they fail to deliver, fold, or break the contract, the code is released to you. It does not give you a working product. It does stop the whole investment from disappearing with the company.\nNavigating the Legal Fallout: Breach of Contract and Fraud If the money is already spent, there are two routes, and both are slow.\nBreach of contract is the usual one. If your statement of work named the features and the dates, and the vendor missed them, you can sue to recover the fees and the costs of the failed rollout. How well that goes depends almost entirely on how specific that document was.\nFraudulent misrepresentation is the harder route. It applies if the vendor knew the software did not exist and had no way to build it. You have to prove what they knew, which is difficult. Damages can be higher, but so are the costs and the time.\nNeither route gets the work done while it runs. Plan the operational fallback in parallel with the legal one.\nConclusion: Procurement as a Defensive Strategy The contract is not paperwork at the end of the process. It is the only part of the purchase you control.\nRefuse the standard terms. Tie money to tests your own team runs. Name the dates and what missing them costs. A vendor with working software will accept all three. A vendor without it will start explaining why those terms are unusual — which is the most useful thing you will learn in the whole negotiation.\nConclude our series and learn how to align procurement with real operational needs in Part 6: Building Genuine Resilience: Choosing Proven SCM Technologies Over Vaporware.\nFrequently Asked Questions (FAQ) What is a \u0026ldquo;Safe Harbor\u0026rdquo; statement and why is it dangerous? It is a disclaimer saying that a vendor\u0026rsquo;s statements about the future are not promises, and that you should only buy on what exists today. It is dangerous because the sales pitch is usually about the future, and this clause says the pitch does not count.\nCan we sue a vendor for the \u0026ldquo;Opportunity Cost\u0026rdquo; of buying vaporware? Rarely with success. Direct costs such as fees and consulting are recoverable. Lost sales or lost market position are hard to prove, and most vendor contracts exclude that kind of loss outright.\nWhy is it important to tie payments to milestones? It moves the risk back to the vendor. If the software cannot pass your test, the vendor does not get paid, and the spending stops before it grows.\nWhat should be included in a Statement of Work (SOW)? Specifics, not marketing. Which records the software reads, from which system, in what volume, how fast, and exactly what it must return. Who tests it, and what counts as a pass. The more precise the document, the easier it is to show a breach.\nHow do we test a feature before a payment is released? Write the test before the contract is signed, and put it in the contract. Use your own data and your own people. Run the test on the version the vendor delivers, not on a demo environment they control.\n"
    tags[3]:
      - "Vaporware"
      - "SCM"
      - "Procurement"
  - title: "Operational Paralysis: When Phantom SCM Software Breaks the Supply Chain"
    permalink: "https://runink.org/blog/vaporware-part-4-operational/"
    date: "2026-06-20T08:28:51Z"
    category: "blog"
    description: "Part of the 6-part series on the dangers of SCM Vaporware."
    content: " This is Part 4 of our 6-part series on the dangers of SCM Vaporware. Read Part 3: Calculating the True Cost of SCM Vaporware for the financial side, and Part 5: Defending Against SCM Vaporware for what to put in the contract.\nPart 3 covered the money. This part covers the freight.\nA supply chain runs on data that arrives on time. When the software meant to move that data does not work, goods stop moving in ways you can see on the dock. This post walks through where the damage shows up: visibility, stock, partner links, manual work and the ability to react.\nThe Vacuum of Data: Navigating Blindly Supply chain software is sold on one promise. You will know where everything is.\nNow consider what happens when it does not work. Most buyers start switching off the old system before the new one is live. Maintenance contracts lapse. The team that knew the old reports moves on. Then the go-live date slips, and slips again, and there is nothing in between.\nManagers go back to email, spreadsheets and phone calls to find a shipment. That is slower, but the real cost is reaction time. A port strike or a storm needs a decision the same day. Finding out on Friday what happened on Tuesday is how a small delay becomes a missed season.\nThe Inventory Bullwhip Effect Amplified Buying decisions need a demand number. Vendors sell algorithms that promise to end both stockouts and excess stock.\nWhen those algorithms do not work, buying falls back on judgement and a spreadsheet. Some buyers will over-order to be safe. Others will under-order because their manual maths was wrong.\nBoth directions cost. Over-ordering fills the warehouse, runs up holding cost and spoils anything perishable. Under-ordering empties the shelf and loses the sale. This is the bullwhip effect: each tier adds its own caution, and the swings get larger the further upstream you go.\nThe Disintegration of the Partner Network A supply chain is not one company. It is suppliers, factories, forwarders and last-mile carriers, connected by EDI messages and APIs.\nWhen the promised connections do not exist, the messages stop.\nSuppliers do not get purchase orders, so production waits. Freight forwarders do not get customs paperwork in time, so containers sit at the port. Warehouses do not get advance shipping notices, so the receiving dock works blind. Each of those lands on a partner who did not choose your software. That is the part that takes longest to repair.\nReverting to the \u0026ldquo;Swivel Chair\u0026rdquo; Interface The most demoralising part is manual re-entry. The industry calls it the swivel chair: two screens, one person, copying between them.\nIf the software cannot pull from the planning system and push to the warehouse system, someone does it by hand. Analysts hired to improve the operation spend their day retyping shipment data.\nIt is slow, and it introduces errors that cost more than the typing. One wrong digit in a container number or a customs value can hold a shipment for weeks and trigger a fine. The volume of manual work is what sets the pace of the whole operation.\nThe Loss of Agility in a Volatile World Agility means changing sourcing or routing quickly when something goes wrong. It is the thing buyers say they are paying for.\nWhen the software does not work, that ability goes. A tariff change or a closed corridor needs a comparison of alternatives this week. If the data is split across systems and the process is manual, the comparison takes weeks, and by then the choice has been made for you.\nConclusion: The Physical Reality of Phantom Tech Supply chain software does not only sit on a server. It controls where thousands of tonnes of goods go. When it does not work, that shows up on a dock.\nSo the rule is about sequence. Verify that the new system does the work before you switch off the one that does. An old system that works beats a new one that only exists in a demo.\nAsk for the function in front of you, with your own data, before anything is decommissioned.\nFor what to put in the contract, read Part 5: Legal Recourse and Contractual Armor: Defending Against SCM Vaporware.\nFrequently Asked Questions (FAQ) How does software that does not work cause stockouts? It leaves buyers without a trustworthy stock or demand number. They either reorder late or order the wrong quantity from a manual estimate. Both end in empty shelves.\nWhat is the \u0026ldquo;bullwhip effect\u0026rdquo; and how does bad software worsen it? Small changes in retail demand turn into larger swings in wholesale, distributor and factory orders. Without shared, accurate data, each tier orders extra to protect itself, and the swings grow.\nWhy do integrations fail with vaporware? Integrations need working, documented APIs. If the vendor has not built the back end, the endpoints either do not exist or fail under load. Ask to call them with your own data before signing.\nCan manual workarounds replace supply chain software temporarily? For a small, local operation, briefly. At volume they do not hold: error rates rise and the pace of the operation drops to the speed of typing.\nHow do you keep goods moving while a system is being replaced? Keep the system that works running until the new one has been shown to do the job with your own data. Write the test into the contract, and set the decommission date after it passes, not before.\n"
    tags[3]:
      - "Vaporware"
      - "SCM"
      - "Procurement"
  - title: "Spotting the Illusion: How to Identify Vaporware in SCM Vendor Pitches"
    permalink: "https://runink.org/blog/vaporware-part-2-spotting/"
    date: "2026-06-20T08:28:51Z"
    category: "blog"
    description: "Part of the 6-part series on the dangers of SCM Vaporware."
    content: " This is Part 2 of our 6-part series on the dangers of SCM Vaporware. Read Part 1: Unmasking the Dangers of Vaporware in Supply Chain Management for a foundational overview, and explore Part 3: Calculating the True Cost of SCM Vaporware for deep financial analysis.\nSupply chain software is sold in rooms where nobody can check the claims. The slides are good, the marketing budget is large, and the promises are about what the product will do rather than what it does. Some of those products are vaporware: software that is marketed heavily but does not exist in a working, installable form.\nYou want to know that before you sign a multi-year contract, not after. That takes a procurement team, a supply chain lead and someone from IT who are all willing to ask the same question twice. This post sets out the questions and the signs.\nThe Anatomy of a Vaporware Pitch A vaporware pitch does not look like a scam. It looks like the future.\nIt is built to work on an executive who is worried about being left behind. The pitch stays on the vision: a supply chain that runs itself, sees disruption coming, and costs less. The supporting material is conceptual animation, a scripted click-through that looks like working software, and a list of the words everyone is using this year — autonomous, self-healing, end-to-end, hyper-automated.\nNone of that is evidence. The shift a buyer has to make is from wanting the newest thing to wanting the thing you can test.\nRed Flag 1: The \u0026ldquo;Sandbox Evasion\u0026rdquo; Tactic The clearest sign is a vendor who will not give you unscripted access to a working test environment.\nMost pitches run as a controlled demonstration. The moment to watch is when your team asks to take the mouse, load your own messy data, and try the cases that break things. A vaporware vendor will not let that happen.\nThe excuses are familiar:\n\u0026ldquo;The software is too customized for your industry to show in a generic sandbox.\u0026rdquo; \u0026ldquo;We can\u0026rsquo;t grant backend access — the AI models are proprietary.\u0026rdquo; \u0026ldquo;The environment is mid-migration and unstable right now.\u0026rdquo; If a product is ready to sell, it can be tested. A vendor who controls every click and will not let your IT team try to break the software in a test environment is showing you a mockup.\nRed Flag 2: AI as a Magic Word Artificial intelligence, machine learning and generative AI are the most abused terms in supply chain marketing. A vaporware vendor uses \u0026ldquo;AI\u0026rdquo; to close any question it cannot answer.\nSo ask the question again, narrowly. If the pitch says the platform uses predictive AI to reroute freight:\nWhich models? Name them — random forests, neural networks, gradient boosting. What was the training data, where did it come from, and how much of it was there? What happens when the world moves away from that training data, or a canal closes? If nobody in the room — including the sales engineers — can answer, and the conversation keeps returning to autonomous decision-making in the abstract, you are probably looking at rules-based logic with a new label on it.\nRed Flag 3: Lack of Peer-Reviewed, Live Case Studies Working software leaves traces. People use it, complain about it and talk about it.\nWhen you ask for references, watch for case studies about the vendor\u0026rsquo;s older product, or testimonials that never quite say the customer uses the feature being sold to you.\nAsk for three things instead:\nDirect access to current users. Speak to a supply chain manager at another company, without the vendor in the room, who uses the exact feature you are buying, daily. Proof of scale. If the claim is tens of thousands of transactions a minute, ask to see that volume measured at a live customer. Care with \u0026ldquo;design partner\u0026rdquo;. If the vendor says the product is new and you would be a strategic design partner, you are funding their development. That can be a fair deal. It is not the purchase of a finished product, and it should not be priced as one. Red Flag 4: The Roadmap that Never Ends Vaporware sells the future. The product today is thin, but the roadmap shows everything you need arriving next year.\nEvery real software company has a roadmap, so the roadmap itself proves nothing. What tells you something is the history. Ask for the roadmaps from the past three years, and check them against what shipped. Then ask for the release notes for the last twelve months. If the notes show interface tweaks and bug fixes while the roadmap promises a rebuilt platform in six months, the engineering to do that is not there. The roadmap is a sales document.\nHow to Interrogate SCM Software Vendors: The Definitive Checklist Five things to do in every evaluation:\nThe unscripted demo. Sixty minutes, no script, your team driving, your own data loaded. The technical meeting. Not only account executives. Ask for the architects and the data scientists, and ask them how the system gets its data and how fresh that data is. Reference checking you control. Cross-check the vendor\u0026rsquo;s testimonials against Gartner, G2 and industry forums. Find users yourself on LinkedIn and ask how the installation actually went. The financial check. Look at the vendor\u0026rsquo;s funding and its spending. Heavy marketing spend with little visible adoption is a reason to ask more questions. Service levels with teeth. Put uptime, integration dates and feature delivery in the contract, with money attached. A vendor who will not accept penalties for missing a date does not expect to hit it. Conclusion: Buying on Evidence Spotting vaporware is a change in how you buy, not a checklist you run once. It means staying skeptical through a good presentation, and treating the buzzwords as decoration rather than information.\nAsk for proof that the software works. Test it yourself. Make the technical people answer technical questions. Those three habits are what keeps a budget, and a year of your operations team\u0026rsquo;s time, from going into something that was never built.\nReady to understand the true financial impact of making the wrong choice? Continue reading Part 3: The Financial Sinkhole: Calculating the True Cost of SCM Vaporware.\nFrequently Asked Questions (FAQ) What is the most common sign that an SCM software pitch is vaporware? The vendor will not let your team use a live test environment, unscripted and hands-on. If all you can get is video and a controlled click-through, take it that the software does not yet work.\nHow do vaporware vendors use AI to trick buyers? They use \u0026ldquo;machine learning\u0026rdquo; and \u0026ldquo;generative AI\u0026rdquo; to cover simple, fixed rules. The method depends on the buyer not asking which models are used, what data trained them, and what happens when conditions change.\nWhy do legitimate-seeming companies sell vaporware? Investors want growth and new products. A company may announce something before it is built to test demand, raise money, or stop customers leaving for a competitor that already has the feature.\nCan reading release notes really expose vaporware? Yes. Software that is actively built has a dense, regular history of updates, fixes and small additions. If the platform is described as advanced but the release notes are thin or missing for a year, the product is not moving.\nWhat question should the buying team ask itself after a vendor meeting? One question: which of the things we just heard could we test next week? Separate the claims you could verify with your own data from the ones that only exist on a slide. Buy on the first list.\n"
    tags[3]:
