      - "Procurement"
  - title: "The Hidden Threat: Unmasking the Dangers of Vaporware in Supply Chain Management (SCM)"
    permalink: "https://runink.org/blog/vaporware-part-1-hidden-threat/"
    date: "2026-06-20T08:28:51Z"
    category: "blog"
    description: "Part of the 6-part series on the dangers of SCM Vaporware."
    content: "This is Part 1 of our 6-part series on the dangers of SCM Vaporware. The full series:\nPart 1: The Hidden Threat: Unmasking the Dangers of Vaporware (Current) Part 2: Spotting the Illusion: How to Identify Vaporware in SCM Vendor Pitches Part 3: The Financial Sinkhole: Calculating the True Cost of SCM Vaporware Part 4: Operational Paralysis: When Phantom SCM Software Breaks the Supply Chain Part 5: Legal Recourse and Contractual Armor: Defending Against SCM Vaporware Part 6: Building Genuine Resilience: Choosing Proven SCM Technologies Over Vaporware Supply chain teams buy a lot of software. Forecasting tools, warehouse automation, tracking, trace systems. Some of it works. Some of it was never built.\nVaporware is software that is announced, marketed and sometimes sold, but never finished or released. In supply chain management it tends to arrive dressed as a big idea: full visibility end to end, forecasts that never miss, a network that repairs itself. The demo exists. The product does not.\nBuying it is not a delay. It is a hole in your plan where a system was supposed to be. This article sets out what that costs, and how to check a vendor before you sign.\nA note on method: there are no figures in this article. We have no survey of our own to cite, and a number invented to make the point would be the same trick the article is about.\nThe Allure of Vaporware: Why Do SCM Leaders Fall for It? Why do experienced buyers sign for something that does not exist?\nBecause the pressure is real. Shortages, port closures, tariff changes and weather all land on the same team. When a vendor offers to make that go away, the offer is hard to refuse.\nVaporware is usually well dressed. Clean screens, convincing charts, and the words of the moment — generative AI, digital twin, hyper-automation. Nobody wants to be the firm that said no and then watched a rival pull ahead. So the decision gets made on the deck rather than on the software.\nThe Tangible Costs of Intangible Tech: The Dangers of Acquiring Vaporware When a promised system never arrives, the damage spreads past the invoice. Here is where it shows up.\n1. Money spent with nothing to show Enterprise supply chain software is expensive, and the licence is only the start. Add the consultants, the internal project team and the training. None of it returns anything if the software never works.\nThe sunk-cost trap then does the rest. The vendor says the feature is one release away, so the spending continues. Meanwhile the systems that do work are not being fixed, because the budget is committed elsewhere.\nTo see the real size of this for your own firm, add up what you have paid a vendor to date and what you have spent internally beside it. Then ask what you would have fixed with the same money.\n2. Work that falls back to spreadsheets A supply chain runs on handovers. Orders move from buying to the warehouse to the carrier, and each step needs the record from the last one.\nWhen the new system does not arrive, those handovers go back to email and spreadsheets. It is worse than before the project started, because the old tools were often switched off or left to rot in the expectation of the new one. The promised links to your ERP, your warehouse system and your third-party logistics providers never get built. What follows is familiar: stock in the wrong place, shipments nobody can trace, and safety stock held up to cover the gap.\n3. Standing still while others move While your team waits for a release date, rivals are using plainer software that works today.\nBy the time the project is called off, years can pass. That time does not come back. Your costs stay where they were, and the bar your customers expect keeps rising.\n4. Lost trust inside the building Rolling out a new supply chain system takes real effort from IT, the warehouse and the buying team. People learn new steps, clean up old records, and change how they work.\nWhen the software turns out to be an illusion, they go back to the old way, and they remember. Leaders who signed for it lose standing with their board. The next project — a sensible, smaller one — is then much harder to fund. That is the part of the cost that lasts longest.\n5. Tied to a vendor by its own roadmap Vaporware vendors are good at roadmaps. Each new one pushes the promised feature out by a quarter, and each one is detailed enough to sound like progress.\nSo the buyer waits. Walking away means admitting the purchase was wrong, often in public, and possibly in court. The roadmap is what keeps the customer in place, and it costs the vendor nothing to print another one.\nHow to Spot Vaporware Before Signing the Contract The answer is not a better slide review. It is asking for proof. Five things to insist on:\nA live system you drive yourself. No recorded demo, no guided script. Ask for an account and try the feature you are buying. If the vendor says the product is too complex to show live, that is the answer. References using the exact feature. Not the vendor\u0026rsquo;s older product, and not a friendly logo. Ask to speak to a customer using the version and the feature you are buying, today. If nobody is using it in the wild, assume it is not finished. The release history. Working software leaves a trail: releases, fixes, small changes, month after month. Ask for the release notes for the past year and a half. A gap in that trail is a gap in the product. Payments tied to delivery. Work with counsel who knows software contracts. Tie money to named features passing your own test, on a named date, with a way out if they do not. Do not pay it all up front. Marketing that outruns the product. Heavy advertising, a huge stand at the trade show, and no customer you can find is a pattern worth noticing. Part 2 goes into the pitch itself in more detail. The Role of AI in Modern SCM: Separating Fact from Fiction AI is the easiest thing to claim and the hardest for a buyer to check. That makes it the most common cover for vaporware.\nThe claims to be careful with are the open-ended ones: predictive AI, self-driving decisions, learns your network. A plain statistical model can be described in all three ways, and often is.\nYou do not need to be a data scientist to test it. Ask four questions. What data does the model read, and where does it come from? What does it output — a number, a ranking, a draft action? How was it checked, and against what? What does it do when the data is missing or late? A team that has built the thing answers those in plain terms. A team that has not will change the subject.\nConclusion: Safeguarding Your Supply Chain Ecosystem Supply chains do need better software. That is why the pitch works.\nThe defence is not cynicism about technology. It is a habit: ask to see it working, ask who else is using it, ask what happens when it fails, and tie the money to the answer. Buy the smaller thing that exists over the larger thing that is coming.\nReady to learn how to identify these phantom platforms before signing a contract? Continue reading Part 2: Spotting the Illusion: How to Identify Vaporware in SCM Vendor Pitches.\nFrequently Asked Questions (FAQ) What is the literal definition of vaporware in business software? Vaporware is software or hardware that is announced and promoted, sometimes sold, but late, never built, or quietly cancelled. In supply chain buying it usually means paying for a feature that exists as a mockup or a slide, not as working software.\nHow does acquiring vaporware directly affect supply chain resilience? Resilience comes from seeing a problem early and being able to act on it. Vaporware gives you neither. When a disruption lands, the team is back on phone calls and spreadsheets, doing by hand the work the software was bought to do.\nCan purchasing vaporware lead to formal legal action against the vendor? Yes. Selling software that does not exist, or misstating what it does, can be a breach of contract and in some cases fraud. Those cases are slow and expensive, and winning one does not get the work done in the meantime. Part 5 covers the contract terms that help.\nWhat is the core difference between vaporware and beta software? Beta software exists. It is an early version, released to a few users, with known faults. You can run it and see what it does. Vaporware cannot be run at all, but is sold as finished.\nWhat should we do if we suspect we have already bought vaporware? Write down what was promised, where it was promised, and what has been delivered against it. Set a date by which the named features must pass a test you define. Stop further payments until then, and start planning for the case where they do not arrive.\n"
    tags[3]:
      - "Vaporware"
      - "SCM"
      - "Procurement"
  - title: "Modal Shift and Route Optimization in Rail-Road Networks"
    permalink: "https://runink.org/blog/runink-modal-shift-optimization/"
    date: "2026-06-15T10:07:34Z"
    category: "blog"
    description: "How a rail-versus-road comparison is actually made, where rail-truck handovers break down, and what a routing engine can and cannot tell you."
    content: "What are the Key Takeaways from this Executive Summary? Quick answer\nA modal shift decision is a comparison. For one consignment, on one lane, you weigh the rail option against the road option on the things the shipper cares about: how long it takes, how reliable it is, what it costs and what it emits. The data for that comparison sits in different systems — rail timetables, road conditions, how long trailers wait at the terminal — and each is read at a different time. So the comparison gets made once, at planning, and is not made again when the day changes. Modal shift is a comparison, not a policy. It holds for the consignment, the lane and the day it was made for. Rail-truck handovers are where multimodal plans come apart. Congestion and waiting time at the handover often cost more than the modal choice saved. The cost of re-planning is the real constraint. Most operations have the data to re-run the decision at midday. What they do not have is the hours to re-plan a day by hand. Where rail meets road, capacity goes to waste and costs rise. Trailers wait. Slots go unfilled. Recent disruption on key sea routes has also made the choice a continuity question, not only a cost one: if one route closes, what else can carry the load?\nThis article is about where that choice is actually made, what data it needs, and why nobody goes back to it once the day has started.\nHow Does Understanding the Transport Optimization Challenge Impact Your Strategy? Quick answer\nIt tells you which problem you are solving. Most operations do not have a modal strategy problem. They have a re-planning problem: the plan was right last night and nobody has the hours to check it against today. Knowing which of the two you face decides whether you need better data or more time. Shipowners have warned about rising risk on major channels, including the Gulf. For shippers, that turns overland options from a filing-cabinet contingency into something worth pricing.\nThe plan then meets two older problems. Handover points between rail and road run slowly. And the switch between modes is coordinated across teams who do not share a system. Both add delay, and delay is where the cost shows up.\nSmaller operators and large ones hit the same wall. The data they would need is recorded. Reading it together, on the day, is the part nobody has time for.\nWhat Does Automated Route Optimization and Modal-Shift Analysis Actually Involve? Quick answer\nA route request is narrow. You give it a start, an end and the limits that apply to that leg. It returns a road distance, a travel time and the line on the map. A modal-shift analysis is the same request run against more than one mode, so the two answers can be set side by side on measured distance and time rather than on habit. The inputs are not exotic. Rail timetables, road conditions, the hours a terminal is open, the limits on the consignment itself — all of it is written down somewhere already.\nThe difficulty is that it is written down in systems that were never built to be read together, and each one is current at a different hour of the day.\nDynamic Decision-Making Systems A route planned the night before is the most careful decision of the day. It is also the decision made with the least information anyone will have all day. The gate queue, the road closure, the refused delivery, the driver running an hour down — none of it has happened yet.\nCheap route requests do not make any single answer better. They change how often you can ask. The planner\u0026rsquo;s question stops being \u0026ldquo;is it worth re-planning the day\u0026rdquo; and becomes \u0026ldquo;what does this leg look like now\u0026rdquo;.\nRail-Truck Interchange Efficiency The handover between rail and road is usually the weakest link in the chain. Time spent waiting there decides whether the modal choice upstream was worth making.\nIt is also the figure least likely to be measured. The railway can see its leg. The carrier can see the road leg. Neither can see the handover.\nIdle time at the handover leaves the books in disguise: as overtime, as a missed window, as a second trip. Each lands in a different budget, and none is labelled with the reason. That is why waiting time at the handover gets argued about rather than measured.\nReal-Time Modal-Shift Analysis Modal shift means moving freight from one mode to another — road to rail, for instance. It pays when it is judged per consignment. It stops paying when it becomes a standing rule.\nThe four criteria are cost, speed, reliability and emissions, and they do not move together. The rail leg that wins on emissions often loses on the delivery window.\nRunink FACE\u0026rsquo;s route capability answers the narrow part of this. It returns a measured road distance, a travel time and the route line for one leg. It does not print a cost or a saving beside them, because the routing provider does not return one. A figure nobody measured, placed next to two that were, is how an estimate gets quoted back as a fact. The money comparison between rail and road stays with the planner and the rate table.\nWhat Does a Modal-Shift Programme Have to Get Right? Quick answer\nFour things, and they pull against each other: how full the rail slots are, landed cost, emissions and the delivery window. A programme that tracks only one will improve that one at the expense of the other three. That is the usual reason these programmes are dropped in their second year. Modal Utilisation An empty rail slot on a lane is a real cost. But it is the carrier\u0026rsquo;s cost until your contract says otherwise.\nSo before you treat it as a lever, find out whose balance sheet an empty slot lands on under the agreements you have signed. The answer is often not the one in the business case.\nLanded Cost The comparison that matters is landed cost per consignment, not the line-haul rate per mode. Landed cost includes the drayage — the short truck move at each end — plus waiting time at the handover, your exposure to detention charges, and the cost of the window you missed.\nRate-only comparisons favour rail more often than landed-cost comparisons do.\nEmissions The emissions difference between rail and road is one of the better documented figures in this field, which is why modal shift appears in so many reduction plans.\nUse a published emissions factor, and write down which one you used. A figure whose source is not recorded will not survive an assurance review.\nThe Delivery Window Reliability breaks modal shift in practice, not average transit time.\nA mode that is slower but consistent can be planned around. A mode that is quicker on average but occasionally very late cannot. The late tail is where expedite costs live.\nThree Situations Where the Question Comes Up Quick answer\nRisk on a sea route, congestion at a handover, and an emissions rule. All three force the same comparison: rail against road, for this consignment, now. All three are usually answered from the last plan rather than from current data, because re-running the comparison costs planner hours nobody has. Scenario 1: Risk on a Maritime Channel Concerns in the Gulf and elsewhere have made overland options worth pricing rather than assuming.\nThe work is unglamorous. Find which consignments could physically go overland. Price the legs. Then find out how much capacity you could actually book at short notice. That last answer is what tells you whether the contingency is real.\nScenario 2: Congestion at a Rail-Truck Interchange When a handover point congests, you have two options: a different hub, or a different time slot.\nBoth need current waiting times at the other hubs. Most shippers learn those after the fact, from the drayage invoice. Measuring waiting time before the next peak is usually worth more than any clever re-routing built on stale figures.\nScenario 3: Emissions Mandates Reporting rules increasingly ask for a figure per shipment, not an annual estimate.\nThat makes the modal record itself the thing you file: which mode carried which consignment, over what distance, with a named emissions factor applied. If you cannot rebuild that record per shipment, the reporting will be hard however well you shift modes.\nWhat Makes a Modal Strategy Survive the Next Five Years? Quick answer\nBeing able to run the comparison again. A modal strategy fixed in a spreadsheet is correct on the day it is built and decays from then on. The version that lasts is a repeatable comparison, cheap enough to re-run when a rate changes, a lane congests or a rule tightens. Networks get more complicated, not less.\nSo any modal decision written down as a fixed rule — \u0026ldquo;this lane goes by rail\u0026rdquo; — will be wrong at some point, and will stay wrong, because nobody is assigned to notice.\nThe answer is not a bigger planning model. It is making the comparison cheap enough that the planner can afford to ask again, and keeping a record of what was compared so the next person can see why the current arrangement exists.\nConclusion Modal shift and route re-planning are not analytics problems. The data is already there, in rail timetables, carrier feeds, terminal systems and the rate table. What is missing is the time to read it together, and the habit of recording which comparison produced the current plan.\nTwo places to start. Measure waiting time at the handover, because it decides whether the modal choice paid. And build the landed-cost comparison per consignment, instead of the line-haul rate comparison per mode.\nBoth are measurement jobs before they are software jobs. If you cannot state your handover waiting time or your landed cost per consignment today, those two numbers are the first thing to produce. They belong to your operation, not to any vendor.\n"
    tags[7]:
