      - "Temperature Control"
      - "Compliance"
      - "Runink"
  - title: "Yard Management Systems: Why Your Distribution Center Cannot Afford to Operate Without One"
    permalink: "https://runink.org/blog/yard-management-system-operations/"
    date: "2026-03-16T02:37:03Z"
    category: "blog"
    description: "What a Yard Management System records, where the cost of a blind yard actually sits, and the two figures to measure before you choose one."
    content: "What are the Key Takeaways from this Executive Summary? Quick answer\nA Yard Management System, or YMS, records every trailer, truck and dock door in the yard. It replaces the clipboard and the two-way radio with a gate check-in, a live dock schedule and a trailer location you can look up. The step beyond recording is using the yard\u0026rsquo;s own history to flag the trailers and doors heading for trouble, instead of reporting on them afterwards. A blind yard pays for itself in detention and demurrage — the charges a carrier adds when you hold its trailer or container past the free time in your contract. Most sites cannot say how much they paid last quarter. The yard is the gap between your transport and warehouse systems. Both record what should happen. Neither records what the yard actually did. Two figures come before any product choice: your average gate-to-gate time last quarter, and the detention you paid against it. What Exactly Is a Yard Management System and Why Should Operations Leaders Care? Quick answer\nA YMS records what happens in the yard: the arrival at the gate, the staging spot, the dock assignment, each trailer move, and the departure. It replaces yard walks, radio calls and a dock schedule kept in a spreadsheet with one record everyone reads. If you run a busy site with fifty dock doors or more, the yard is the least controlled ground in your whole operation.\nTrailers arrive unannounced. Drivers wait without a dock. Yard drivers look for empties from memory and radio chatter. Waiting time grows, carriers bill for it, and the appointment schedule exists on paper but not in fact.\nA YMS writes all of it down. A truck hits the gate, the system logs the arrival, checks it against the booking, and gives it a staging spot or a door based on what the load is and how full the yard is. The yard driver gets the task on a handheld rather than over the radio. Every move is time-stamped and located.\nFor whoever runs the yard, the point is simple. You stop guessing where trailers are. You stop paying carriers for time your own delay created. And your warehouse and transport systems finally get the yard data they were never built to capture.\nWhat Is the Real Cost of Not Having a YMS? Quick answer\nFour costs, and they sit in four different budgets: detention and demurrage charges, dock doors standing idle, safety incidents from untracked trailer moves, and carriers quietly pricing your site as a difficult one. None of these is hypothetical. They are line items, just not in the same place.\nDetention and demurrage. The most visible one. Free time runs out quickly when a truck sits in a queue because nobody knows which door is about to open. Check the detention rate in your own carrier contracts, multiply by the hours you were billed for last quarter, and you have the figure. CSCMP\u0026rsquo;s annual State of Logistics report has identified detention as one of the fastest-growing cost categories in domestic freight.\nIdle dock doors. A site without a YMS usually cannot say how much of its door capacity it actually uses, because nobody records when a door was free. Operations that do well on this manage the yard as an asset rather than as a car park, and a utilisation target is meaningless until you know your own starting point. The first step is measuring it: log when each door was occupied for a fortnight, by hand if you have to.\nSafety. Unauthorised trailer moves, reversing into an occupied door, and people walking where vehicles are moving all become more likely when nobody knows what is where. Those incidents carry direct costs in compensation and indirect costs in downtime. Both belong in the business case, and both are usually left out.\nCarrier goodwill. Drivers who wait a long time at your site talk, and so do their dispatchers. At your next tender, the bids will quietly include a premium for a difficult site. You will never see it itemised, and you will pay it.\nHow Does Yard Prediction Go Beyond Basic YMS Tracking? Quick answer\nRecording tells you where a trailer is now. Prediction uses the yard\u0026rsquo;s own arrival and waiting history to say which doors will congest, which trailer should move next, and which trailers have been forgotten. It raises specific trailers for attention rather than displaying all of them. A YMS is a record. That alone is a big step up from a whiteboard. The next step is turning that record into a short list of things to deal with.\nExpected congestion. Past arrival patterns, carrier punctuality and the current state of the yard together say when the pressure will come. That is enough to move yard drivers and clear staging lanes before the queue forms rather than after.\nWhich trailer moves next. Better than first-in, first-out. Rank the waiting trailers by what is on them, what they are committed to deliver, how perishable it is, and how close the carrier is to the end of its free time. The ranking is a suggestion for a yard supervisor to confirm or overrule.\nThings nobody noticed. A trailer that has not moved in days. A carrier whose arrivals are drifting later week by week. A door that consistently takes longer per load than the ones beside it. All three are in the data already, and none of them shows up on a screen full of current positions.\nMIT\u0026rsquo;s Center for Transportation \u0026amp; Logistics researches modelling a site as a live digital copy of itself. That is the idea underneath this: treat the yard as something with a state and a history, not as a parking area with a gate.\nHow Do You Know If Your Facility Is Ready for a YMS? Quick answer\nReadiness is about pain, not size. If your yard manager\u0026rsquo;s tools are a whiteboard, a radio and a spreadsheet updated twice a shift, the case is already made. If finance is writing off detention as a cost of doing business, it was made a while ago. A rough test: busy gate, more doors than one person can watch, and a recurring detention bill. If all three are true, a YMS will pay attention to something nobody currently owns.\nThe connection work is well trodden. A YMS reads your warehouse system for what is arriving and leaving, your transport system for booked appointments, and your gate equipment for check-in. Most warehouse and transport systems already offer the interfaces needed.\nWhat separates a useful rollout from an expensive one is what you do with the data afterwards. Knowing where trailers are is the floor. Using the record — with carrier punctuality, your throughput pattern and who is actually on shift — to decide what moves next is where it starts paying.\nConclusion Quick answer\nA yard is the least recorded part of most operations, and every load passes through it. A YMS closes that gap. Before choosing one, measure your gate-to-gate time and the detention you paid against it. The yard is the last mile of the first mile. Every shipment in or out goes through it, and it is the part nobody instruments.\nA worthwhile first step, before any product decision: your average gate-to-gate time for last quarter, and the detention you paid against it. Most operations cannot produce either figure, which is itself the finding. Talk to us if it would help to work them out.\nIf your detention bill is rising, your doors sit idle at the wrong times, and your yard drivers navigate by memory, the question is not whether you need a YMS. It is how many more quarters of that you want to pay for first.\nSources Council of Supply Chain Management Professionals (CSCMP) — Annual State of Logistics Report, which identifies detention and demurrage as fast-growing freight cost categories OSHA Warehousing \u0026amp; Distribution Safety Standards — Federal guidance on powered industrial trucks, keeping people and vehicles apart, and yard safety "
    tags[6]:
      - "YMS"
      - "Yard Management"
      - "Dock Scheduling"
      - "Dwell Time"
      - "Detention Fees"
      - "Runink"
  - title: "Freight Audit and Payment Automation — Stop Overpaying Your Carriers"
    permalink: "https://runink.org/blog/freight-audit-payment-automation/"
    date: "2026-03-15T20:23:02Z"
    category: "blog"
    description: "Nobody can tell you what you are overpaying your carriers, including us. Here is how to measure it on your own invoices, and what changes when every line is read against the contracted rate instead of a sample."
    content: "What are the Key Takeaways from this Executive Summary? Quick answer\nWhat a shipper overpays its carriers is knowable, and almost nobody has measured it. The causes are billing errors, duplicate invoices and rate discrepancies that a manual process cannot catch at volume — small enough individually to survive until the dispute window closes. Automating the audit changes what gets examined rather than what gets estimated: every invoice is compared against the contracted rate, so a discrepancy arrives as a named line item with the clause it breaches. Freight billing errors are usually small and individually unremarkable, which is why they survive until the dispute window has closed. A manual audit team can only validate so many invoices a day, so most of the invoice file is paid without anyone checking it against the contract. What you recover depends entirely on what is actually wrong with your own invoices. No supplier can give you that figure before reading them, and one who offers a recovery percentage in advance is describing somebody else\u0026rsquo;s ledger. Why Are Shippers Still Overpaying Carriers by Millions Every Year? Quick answer\nCarrier overpayment persists because freight billing is complicated. Accessorial charges, fuel surcharges, minimum charges, dimensional weight adjustments and multi-stop rate structures all create room for error on every invoice cycle. Most organizations do not have the systems or the headcount to check each line against the contracted tariff, so errors flow straight through to payment. The freight payment problem is not carrier dishonesty. It is complexity at volume. Take a mid-market shipper moving 50,000 shipments a year across FTL, LTL, parcel and intermodal. That can easily produce 200,000 or more invoice line items in a year. Each line carries its own rate logic: base rate, fuel surcharge table, accessorial fees for liftgate, inside delivery, detention or demurrage, and weight or dimensional adjustments that change with every load.\nWe are not going to tell you what you are overpaying. Published percentages circulate for this, attributed to one research house or another, and we cannot get to a report behind any of them — so quoting one here would be borrowing a number rather than citing one.\nThe figure is yours and it is cheap to get. Take one month of carrier invoices. Have one person compare every line against the rate table and the accessorial schedule that governed it — the base rate, the fuel surcharge index for that week, each accessorial actually performed, the weight or dimensional adjustment. Total the variances, divide by what you paid that month, and you have your own rate, on your own contracts, for the cost of a few days\u0026rsquo; work. Do it on a month chosen at random rather than a month somebody flagged.\nThe errors are rarely dramatic. A fuel surcharge calculated on last week\u0026rsquo;s index instead of this week\u0026rsquo;s. A duplicate invoice submitted under two different PRO numbers. An accessorial charge for a liftgate that was never used. Each one is small. Added up across a year of invoices, they are not.\nWhat Does the Freight Audit Process Actually Look Like? Quick answer\nFreight audit has five stages: capturing and normalizing the invoice, validating rates against the contracted tariff, detecting duplicates across carriers and time periods, coding to the general ledger and allocating cost, and executing payment with remittance. Each stage carries its own risk of error when it is done by hand. Any VP of Logistics Finance looking at automation needs the lifecycle first. It breaks into five stages, each with its own workload:\nInvoice capture and normalization. Carrier invoices arrive in dozens of formats: EDI 210s, PDFs, spreadsheets, carrier portal exports and paper. Before anything can be validated, each one has to be turned into the same structure. On a manual team, a large part of the working day goes on parsing and re-keying rather than on checking.\nRate validation. This is the audit. Every charge has to be checked against the rate that governs it — the tariff, the routing guide, the negotiated accessorial schedule. That means matching the origin-destination pair, the weight or pallet count, the service level and the applicable surcharges to the correct version of the contract. With hundreds of carrier contracts, each holding thousands of rate records, doing this by hand across the whole invoice file is not realistic.\nDuplicate detection. Duplicate invoices are more common than most finance teams expect. Carriers resubmit after corrections, bill separately for accessorials already included in the linehaul charge, or submit under a different reference number. Your own rate is a query rather than a benchmark: group last year\u0026rsquo;s paid invoices by carrier, amount and date, and look at the collisions.\nGL coding and cost allocation. Every freight charge has to land on the right general ledger account, cost center, business unit or customer order. Miscoding does not cause an overpayment by itself, but it ruins spend visibility. Transportation cost per unit shipped, cost-to-serve by customer and modal cost comparisons all depend on the coding being right.\nPayment execution. Once validated and coded, the invoice moves to payment. Carrier payment terms, early payment discounts and consolidated remittance across hundreds of carriers add their own work, and it grows with volume.\nWhy Can\u0026rsquo;t Manual Audit Teams Keep Up? Quick answer\nManual freight audit is limited by headcount, by how fast a person can work, and by the amount of rate logic a modern carrier contract contains. Most manual teams audit a sample, so the rest of the invoice file is paid without being checked against the contract. The arithmetic is yours to run, and it only needs two numbers you already have. The first is how many invoices one analyst validates in a day when checking each line against the contract — somewhere around 50 to 75 is a reasonable working figure, and your own team will tell you theirs. The second is how many invoices you receive a month.\nPut 5,000 invoices a month against 50 to 75 a day, and checking every one needs three to four full-time analysts. That assumes no time at all on exception resolution, carrier disputes or reporting, which is not how the month goes. Turn the same sum round and it gives you your own coverage: one analyst at that rate, against that volume, covers part of the file rather than all of it.\nSo most teams audit a sample and estimate an error rate from it. Your own coverage figure is a division you can do this week — invoices validated last month over invoices received last month, from the audit log and the AP file. Whatever the share, the remainder was paid unexamined, and what was wrong in it stays wrong quarter after quarter.\nPeak season makes it worse. When volumes rise in Q4 or during a promotion, the backlog grows rather than the capacity. Invoices age past their dispute windows, and once a carrier\u0026rsquo;s payment terms have run, recovery gets difficult.\nHow Does Automated Freight Audit Change the Equation? Quick answer\nAutomating the audit moves the work from sampling to comparison. Every invoice is read and every line is compared against the rate in the contract that governs it, before payment rather than after, so a discrepancy arrives as a named line item with a variance and a clause attached. Automation is not the manual process run faster. It changes what gets examined. Reading invoices against the contract that governs them is one of the kinds of work Runink FACE does, and five things distinguish it from a quicker manual review:\nEvery invoice, not a sample. Each invoice, each line item and each accessorial charge is compared with the contracted rate. There is no sample to draw and no backlog to work down. A discrepancy is raised before payment rather than found in a quarterly reconciliation months later.\nRate validation against the contract. Contracted rates, fuel surcharge tables and accessorial schedules are loaded once. When an invoice arrives with a rate that does not match, the discrepancy is raised with the dollar variance and the contract clause that applies, so the dispute is already half written.\nDuplicate detection beyond the PRO number. Matching looks for the same charge across different invoice numbers, different submission dates and different carrier divisions — comparisons a reviewer working through invoices one at a time has no way to make.\nPattern detection. Models trained on your historical freight data pick out billing that sits outside the usual pattern: accessorials appearing more often than the lane would suggest, weight discrepancies that point to a dimensioning error, or rate drift on a lane that indicates the contract is not being applied.\nGL coding from shipment attributes. Shipment attributes map to GL codes by configurable rules, so allocation does not depend on anyone re-keying it.\nOne thing these capabilities deliberately do not do is predict the recovery. What an audit program returns is a function of what is actually wrong with your invoices, and that is unknown until they have been read. Any projected recovery quoted before that point is a claim about another shipper\u0026rsquo;s contracts, not about yours.\nConclusion Quick answer\nFreight audit and payment automation is not a back-office efficiency project. It is where a controllable transportation cost either gets checked against the contract or gets paid without anyone looking. Every freight invoice paid without validation is a charge nobody compared with the agreement behind it. Once one month has been checked by hand, the open question stops being whether some of it is wrong. It becomes which lines, on which invoices, and whether the dispute window is still open.\nMoving from a sample to reading every invoice does not produce an estimate. It produces a list: this line, this carrier, this variance, this clause. Rate validation, duplicate detection, pattern flagging and GL coding all run against the whole invoice file rather than the part a team had time for.\nRunink FACE reads every invoice line against the contract that governs it and drafts the dispute where the two do not agree — then hands the draft to whoever signs disputes, because a dispute is a commercial communication and not a notification. Start by establishing one figure of your own: what proportion of last year\u0026rsquo;s freight invoices was checked against the contract by anyone at all. Start a conversation with our team if it would help to work it out.\nSources Council of Supply Chain Management Professionals (CSCMP) — Research on duplicate payment frequency, freight payment practice and transportation cost management frameworks Gartner — Transportation Management Systems Magic Quadrant — Analysis of TMS platform capabilities, including anomaly detection, freight audit integration and billing validation "
